Q: Errors when trying to call Console.WriteLine() with &#39;$&#39; in front of a string A: <p>I think the problem is not string interpolation, it is ok. The problem is using it (it is an expression) as a statement in a method without assignment, which is not legal conceptually. Before <code>Console.WriteLine(...);</code> assign the interpolation string to a variable and then you can use it.</p>
<pre><code>var varname = $&quot;...  &quot;;
</code></pre>

Q: Xamarin.Forms: iOS displays the words &quot;detail&quot; and &quot;master&quot; instead of showing only backbutton and hamburger. Android works fine A: <p>That's just how native iOS behaves you might have to change a couple of things but I can assure you it's a cinch.</p>
<p>First of all, to turn off back button text you add <code>NavigationPage.BackButtonTitle=&quot;&quot;</code> to your <code>ContentPage.</code></p>
<p>Secondly, iOS does not have a hamburger menu icon and hence to get it you have to set this icon manually!</p>
<p>So in your Details <code>NavigationPage</code> you give it an icon source.</p>
<pre><code>var navPage = new NavigationPage(new SomePage());
navPage.Icon = &quot;hamburgerIcon.png&quot;
masterDetailPage.Detail= navPage;
</code></pre>
<p>Where your hamburger icon is the icon that you want to set.</p>

Q: Elementor Free: JavaScript changes tab-specific background image, but disables the default background when page is opened from the main menu A: <p>The Elementor rule showing as crossed out means your own rule is winning the cascade on <code>::before</code>, and it keeps winning on <code>/services</code> too, hash or no hash. A pseudo-element can't be reached from JS at all (<code>element.style</code> only touches the element itself), so an override like that has to sit in a stylesheet with <code>!important</code> - that is why the default image never comes back.</p>
<p>First, stop hardcoding the default and read Elementor's own value. <code>getComputedStyle</code> takes a second argument for pseudo-elements:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const header = document.querySelector('.elementor-element-4842995');
const original = getComputedStyle(header, '::before').backgroundImage;
</code></pre>
<p>See <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/getComputedStyle" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Window.getComputedStyle()</a>.</p>
<p>Then swap through a custom property instead of an <code>!important</code> override. Custom properties inherit, and a pseudo-element inherits from the element that generates it, so setting the variable on the container reaches <code>::before</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.elementor-element-4842995::before { background-image: var(--hdr-bg); }
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>header.style.setProperty('--hdr-bg', original);
header.style.setProperty('--hdr-bg', 'url(&quot;' + img + '&quot;)');
</code></pre>
<p>Inheritance into pseudo-elements is documented in <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Using_CSS_custom_properties" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Using CSS custom properties</a> and <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/::before" rel="nofollow noreferrer">::before</a>.</p>
<p>One more thing for the hash case: hook the widget rather than <code>DOMContentLoaded</code>. Nested Tabs picks its active tab during init, so anything running earlier gets overwritten:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>jQuery(window).on('elementor/frontend/init', function () {
  elementorFrontend.hooks.addAction('frontend/element_ready/nested-tabs.default', function ($scope) {
    // open the tab from location.hash, then set --hdr-bg
  });
});
</code></pre>
<p>The hook names are listed in the <a href="https://developers.elementor.com/docs/scripts-styles/frontend-scripts/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Elementor frontend scripts docs</a>.</p>

Q: How to load a screen within the iOS app using Xamarin.Forms Shell feature for navigation? A: <p>I guess the easy way to do it would be to get the CurrentViewController from the currently displayed window.</p>
<pre><code>public UIViewController GetCurrentUIViewController()
    {
        UIViewController viewController;
        var window = UIApplication.SharedApplication.KeyWindow;
        if (window == null)
        {
            return null;
        }

        if (window.RootViewController.PresentedViewController == null)
        {
            window = UIApplication.SharedApplication.Windows
                     .First(i =&gt; i.RootViewController != null &amp;&amp;
                                 i.RootViewController.GetType().FullName
                                 .Contains(typeof(Xamarin.Forms.Platform.iOS.Platform).FullName));
        }

        viewController = window.RootViewController;

        while (viewController.PresentedViewController != null)
        {
            viewController = viewController.PresentedViewController;
        }

        return viewController;
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Then use it where you need it :</p>
<pre><code>var currentViewController= GetCurrentUIViewController();
currentViewController?.PresentViewController(responseVC, true, null);
</code></pre>
